From 5e6b920640da6a0bfd94c0a1c5220f0ffb76a80c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: anthonybartczak <49080651+anthonybartczak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 22:01:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Say the two-frame limit is what was seen, not a rule

No message has been seen in more than two frames, and the realigner
leans on that to abandon an unfinished header. Nothing establishes it
as a limit of the device, so the comment and PROTOCOL.md now say so,
and name the case it leaves open.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 PROTOCOL.md                  | 6 ++++--
 crates/hx-usb/src/realign.rs | 6 +++---
 2 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/PROTOCOL.md b/PROTOCOL.md
index eb436e2..c6e9875 100644
--- a/PROTOCOL.md
+++ b/PROTOCOL.md
@@ -169,8 +169,10 @@ declare at most 4 KB (the device never has more than that unacknowledged,
 above) and whose body is exactly one notification, or the bare service number
 that answers the service opening. Anything else is stepped over a byte at a
 time. So is a header whose message is still missing bytes a frame later, once a
-whole notification has arrived behind it: the device sends a message in two
-frames at most. The cost is the one notification the slip cut through, and
+whole notification has arrived behind it. No message has been seen in more than
+two frames, but nothing establishes that as a limit, so a notification that
+took three and carried another one's bytes as data would lose its header. The
+cost is the one notification the slip cut through, and
 `Session::events_skipped` counts the bytes. The body check is needed: stepping
 through a parameter notification, the bytes `ca 00 00 00 00` (a float 0.0) and
 the key after them pass as a header for service 0 with a small length.
diff --git a/crates/hx-usb/src/realign.rs b/crates/hx-usb/src/realign.rs
index 4eba966..682f06b 100644
--- a/crates/hx-usb/src/realign.rs
+++ b/crates/hx-usb/src/realign.rs
@@ -119,9 +119,9 @@ impl Realign {
                 // The rest may still come, so wait for it. Two things
                 // together show that it will not, and that this header was
                 // not a header: it has already waited through a whole frame
-                // (the device sends a message in two frames at most), and a
-                // whole notification has arrived behind it. Neither is
-                // enough alone. A long message may take more frames, and
+                // (no message has been seen in more than two), and a whole
+                // notification has arrived behind it. Neither is enough
+                // alone. A long message may take more frames, and
                 // what lies behind an unfinished header is inside its
                 // declared body, where the bytes of a notification can turn
                 // up as data. Waiting on regardless would hold back
